{"author":"Joe Oakhart","author_email":"joeoakhart@navapbc.com","data":{"body":"AC-VAGUENESS DEFECT NOW HAS A MEASURED PRECISION COST. Third independent confirmation, and the first where it is dispositive rather than incidental. On carbon the criterion reported the term appropriate as a vague acceptance-criterion term when the word appears once in narrative prose; that row survived on an independent ground, so the defect cost nothing. On uswds a judge classified rebar item 12 as an outright FALSE POSITIVE: it asserts AC and definition-of-done vagueness citing etc, reasonable and sufficient, and a scan of every checklist line in the plan found none of the three in any acceptance criterion - they occur only in Notes, code comments, narrative and files-touched lists at eight scattered locations. Both plans carry verbatim acceptance criteria and neither received scaffolding, so the criterion is reading the whole ticket description rather than the criteria lines it is scoped to. The progression across the three observations is the point: a scoping fault that produced only noisy-but-survivable rows on plans with dense criteria produces a false positive on a plan whose narrative happens to contain the trigger words. Precision cost is therefore input-dependent and will not show up on every corpus."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789447320682592001,"uuid":"a7bd0355-7a97-47b1-ad54-18c8ebda103b"}